<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

Most course pages combine explanatory text with a few CAT-SOOP tags for
structure, reuse, computation, and questions.  The following example shows the
main pieces:

<pre><code class="language-nohighlight">
&lt;python&gt;
cs_content_header = "Homework 1"
topic = "diffusion"
&lt;/python&gt;

This page introduces **\@{topic}** and then asks you to apply it.

&lt;tableofcontents/&gt;

&lt;catsoop-section&gt;Modeling the System&lt;/catsoop-section&gt;

Ordinary prose can use Markdown, including lists, links, mathematics, and
fenced code.

&lt;include&gt;shared/modeling_note.md&lt;/include&gt;

&lt;catsoop-subsection&gt;One Update Step&lt;/catsoop-subsection&gt;

&lt;question pythonic&gt;
csq_name = "one_step"
csq_prompt = "Compute the value after one update."
csq_soln = 0.25
&lt;/question&gt;
</code></pre>

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>Sections and Subsections</catsoop-section>

Use:

```html
<catsoop-section>A Major Topic</catsoop-section>
<catsoop-subsection>A Topic Within It</catsoop-subsection>
```

CAT-SOOP turns these tags into numbered headings, adds link targets, and
includes them in `<tableofcontents/>`.  There is also
`<catsoop-subsubsection>` for a third level.  Prefer these tags to handwritten
`<h2>` and `<h3>` elements when the heading is part of the page's logical
outline.

`<tableofcontents/>` is optional.  Put it near the start of a longer page to
insert links to the CAT-SOOP section headings found later in the page.

<catsoop-section>Including Shared Source</catsoop-section>

`<include>` replaces the tag with one or more files before the rest of the page
is processed:

<pre><code class="language-html">
&lt;include&gt;shared/introduction.md&lt;/include&gt;
</code></pre>

Paths are relative to the directory containing the current page source.  An
include can name a Markdown, HTML, or CAT-SOOP fragment; the included text is
processed as part of the surrounding page.  The file must remain inside the
course directory.

Several files can be included in order, one per line:

<pre><code class="language-html">
&lt;include&gt;
shared/setup.md
shared/example.html
&lt;/include&gt;
</code></pre>

Includes are useful for instructions, feedback questions, figures, or policy
text reused across several pages.  Keep a complete page in `content.*`; use
included files as fragments rather than pages with their own `preload.py`.

<catsoop-section>Running Page Python</catsoop-section>

A `<python>` block executes trusted server-side Python while CAT-SOOP builds
the page:

<pre><code class="language-nohighlight">
&lt;python&gt;
import math

radius = 3
area = math.pi * radius**2
print("&lt;p&gt;The area is %.2f.&lt;/p&gt;" % area)
&lt;/python&gt;
</code></pre>

Printed text is inserted at the location of the tag.  Variables assigned in
one Python block remain in the page context and can be used by later Python or
question blocks.  The short form `\@{expression}` prints one expression inside
the surrounding text.

Page Python runs in the CAT-SOOP server process, not in a student sandbox.  It
is appropriate for selecting or generating content, but it must never execute
student-submitted code or interpolate unescaped request data into HTML.

<catsoop-section>Adding Questions</catsoop-section>

A question block names a qtype and contains Python settings for one question:

<pre><code class="language-nohighlight">
&lt;question multiplechoice&gt;
csq_name = "storage_choice"
csq_prompt = "Which structure supports fast lookup?"
csq_options = ["List", "Dictionary", "Tuple"]
csq_soln = "Dictionary"
&lt;/question&gt;
</code></pre>

The qtype after `<question` determines which settings are available and how the
answer is rendered and graded.  Give every important question a stable,
page-unique `csq_name`; CAT-SOOP uses it when storing submissions and scores.

Question settings are Python, so they can use values and helper functions
defined by `preload.py` or an earlier `<python>` block.  The question block
itself does not display ordinary prose outside its `csq_prompt` and other
qtype-specific fields.

<catsoop-section>Processing Order</catsoop-section>

For a `.catsoop` or `.md` page, the practical order is:

1. parent and page `preload.py` files establish the context;
2. the authenticated user's course information is loaded;
3. `<include>` files are inserted;
4. `<python>` blocks and `\@{...}` expressions run in source order;
5. Markdown and HTML are converted into page markup;
6. `<question>` blocks become interactive CAT-SOOP questions;
7. the page handler records or displays the resulting content.

The separate [Page Rendering and Request Data](COURSE/docs/authoring/rendering)
guide explains this model, mixed Markdown/HTML behavior, and `cs_form` in more
detail.
